// Places for people, to put a road between.
//
//     import 'buildings' { House, Row, Tower }
//
//     object House(4)
//     object Row(5, 'winter')      { translate X 12 }
//     object Tower(14, 'summer', 3) { translate X -14 }
//
// | `House` | One dwelling: plinth, walls, a gabled roof, a door and windows. |
// | `Row` | Several houses joined shoulder to shoulder, as a terrace. |
// | `Tower` | A taller block, bay after bay, with a parapet rather than a roof. |
//
// The three numbers mean what they mean in the other libraries -- **how big**, **what time of year**,
// and **which one of that kind** -- and only the first is required.  Height is the eaves-to-ground
// measure for a house and the whole height for a tower; for a `Row` the first number is **how many
// houses**, since a terrace is counted rather than measured.
//
// **The season puts snow on the roofs** and does nothing else, which is the whole of what winter does
// to a building from the outside.  A building is not deciduous; it takes the word so that a scene
// setting its season once gets roofs that agree with the ground.
//
// -- What makes a box into a building --------------------------------------------------------------
//
// This library exists because of a scene that failed at it.  `a-block-of-buildings` in the gallery
// raises eleven towers out of one loop, and says of itself that they are *boxes with lights on, not
// architecture* -- a real building has a plan, a frontage, and a way of meeting the ground.  Four
// things do most of that work, and all four are cheap:
//
// **A plinth.**  A wall that meets the ground in a line reads as a sheet standing on grass.  A course
// at the bottom, a little proud of the wall and a little lighter, is how a building sits down.
//
// **Depth in the window.**  A rectangle of dark paint on a wall is a picture of a window.  What reads
// is the *reveal*: the glass set back, a frame standing proud around it, and a sill that overhangs and
// throws a small shadow.  The first try here laid bars *across* the opening instead of bordering it,
// and every window came out as a white plus sign stuck to the wall.
//
// **An overhang.**  Eaves that reach past the wall lay a shadow line along the top of the facade.
// Without it a roof looks glued on; with it the roof is clearly above and the wall clearly below.
//
// **A top that is not a cut.**  A gable, or a parapet standing above the roof line.  A box ended flat
// at the top is the single strongest tell that a thing is a box.
//
// The gable is worth a word.  Its pitch is forty-five degrees, and that is not a style choice: it lets
// the end wall be cut as the top half of a square stood on its corner, which is one `intersection` of
// two boxes.  A cube turned a quarter is a *diamond*, not a triangle -- turning one and hoping leaves
// a diamond sticking up through the ridge, which is what the first attempt did.
//
// -- What is here for the library and not for you --------------------------------------------------
//
// Everything beginning with `Build` is the machinery.  The materials arrive in your scene whether you
// asked or not, for the reason the other libraries give -- a material is looked up where it is *used*
// -- so they carry the prefix.

// -- What buildings are made of --------------------------------------------------------------------

BuildWall = material { pigment [0.74, 0.70, 0.62]  specular 0.05  shininess 16 }
BuildWallWarm = material { pigment [0.66, 0.53, 0.44]  specular 0.05  shininess 16 }
BuildWallCool = material { pigment [0.62, 0.63, 0.61]  specular 0.06  shininess 18 }
BuildTrim = material { pigment [0.90, 0.88, 0.84]  specular 0.12  shininess 30 }
BuildRoof = material { pigment [0.30, 0.26, 0.25]  specular 0.10  shininess 20 }
BuildRoofRed = material { pigment [0.42, 0.24, 0.18]  specular 0.10  shininess 20 }
BuildGlass = material { pigment [0.09, 0.12, 0.15]  specular 0.55  shininess 140  reflective 0.22 }
// Brick, and a shade off the walls: a chimney built of the same render as the house reads as a lump of
// the house rather than as a chimney.
BuildChimney = material { pigment [0.34, 0.24, 0.20]  specular 0.03  shininess 8  ambient 0.10 }

BuildDoor = material { pigment [0.28, 0.18, 0.14]  specular 0.20  shininess 40 }
BuildSnow = material { pigment [0.93, 0.94, 0.97]  specular 0.25  shininess 50 }

// Three walls and two roofs, so a row does not come out as one house repeated.  Which one a building
// wears is its variant's business.
primitive BuildWallOf(variant) -> material {
    switch (mod(floor(variant), 3)) {
        case 0 { return material BuildWall }
        case 1 { return material BuildWallWarm }
        default { return material BuildWallCool }
    }
}

// -- Brick -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The brick pattern is laid out in *inches*: its brick is eight long, three high and four and a half
// deep, with a half-inch joint, which is a real brick.  So the only thing to work out is how many
// scene units an inch is, and a two-storey house is twenty feet -- two hundred and forty inches.
// Brick is a real size whatever size the house is, so a taller house gets more courses rather than
// bigger bricks, which is one of the stronger cues that a thing is built rather than modelled.

// Three bricks to go with the three walls: a yellow stock, a red, and a grey.
//
// The mortar is only a shade lighter than its brick, which is what a wall looks like from across a
// street rather than from a foot away.
//
// It is worth recording what that did *not* fix, since the reasoning that led to it was wrong.  These
// walls read pale grey under the gallery's sky, and the theory was that a joint takes up a fifth of a
// wall's face, so mortar much lighter than the brick would average the whole wall towards it.
// Darkening all three mortars moved the walls by **one part in two hundred and fifty-five** -- nothing.
// What makes a sky-lit wall pale is the sky's own light filling in the channels the brick is dark in:
// red brick at 0.48, 0.25, 0.19 comes back as 118, 104, 89, with the green and blue lifted almost to
// the red.  The mortar is still right at this value, but as a close-up nicety, not as a cure.
function BuildBrickTint(variant) -> color {
    switch (mod(floor(variant), 3)) {
        case 0 { return [0.56, 0.50, 0.39] }
        case 1 { return [0.48, 0.25, 0.19] }
        default { return [0.42, 0.39, 0.37] }
    }
}

function BuildMortarTint(variant) -> color {
    switch (mod(floor(variant), 3)) {
        case 0 { return [0.62, 0.60, 0.55] }
        case 1 { return [0.55, 0.52, 0.48] }
        default { return [0.57, 0.56, 0.54] }
    }
}

// Brick for a wall of a given size, which is why this has to be told the size.
//
// **A pattern is worked out in the surface's own space**, and a wall is a unit cube scaled differently
// on all three axes -- so laid on plainly, the same pattern gives long flat bricks on the front and
// short tall ones on the end.  The scale below divides that back out, so `sx`, `sy` and `sz` are
// whatever the cube wearing this was scaled by.  A wall whose material sits on something unscaled --
// the gables are an `intersection` carrying only a translate -- passes ones.
//
// Dividing it back out buys something beyond bricks of one size: both patterns then measure in inches
// from their own origin, and the wall's origin is its middle while the gable's is the wall top, which
// is `height * 0.50` above it.  That is a hundred and twenty inches, or forty courses exactly, so the
// gable's courses carry on from the wall's without a jog at the eave.  It is worth knowing that is
// arithmetic and not luck: change the wall height away from half the house and it stops being true.
//
// `nz` shifts the pattern along Z, and it exists because of a trap.  The pattern lays joints in all
// three directions, so a face of constant Z either misses the depth joints or lands in one -- and if it
// lands in one, *every other course of that whole face turns to mortar*, since the depth joints are
// staggered by course.  A wall six units high is seventy-two inches deep, and seventy-two is exactly
// sixteen bricks, so the front and back faces sit precisely on a joint plane.  What saves them today is
// the epsilon the pattern adds before it compares, which tips an exact landing to brick rather than
// mortar -- far too fine a thing to rest a wall on.  Driving it the other way on purpose was measured:
// sixty-one per cent of the wall came out mortar instead of twenty-two.  So the wall asks for a shift
// of a little under an inch, which puts its faces a third of the way into a brick.  The gables, at
// three and a half inches, are already clear and ask for none.
//
// `ny` shifts it along Y, which is what makes two walls agree about where a course line falls.  The
// wall gets away without one because the gable's forty courses fall out of the proportions; a wall
// whose origin is at some other height has to be told how far, in inches, and the remainder over six
// -- a course and its stagger -- is the number to give.
//
// What none of this can do is turn a corner.  Real brickwork *toothes* at a quoin, alternate courses
// reaching round; this is a lattice cut by the wall's faces, so the courses line up in height all the
// way round and the bond does not interlock at the corner.  At the distance a building is ever seen
// from, that is invisible.
primitive BuildBrickOf(variant, height, sx, sy, sz, ny = 0, nz = 0) -> material {
    inch = height / 240

    return material {
        pigment brick {
            BuildMortarTint(variant), BuildBrickTint(variant)
            scale [inch / sx, inch / sy, inch / sz]
            translate [0, -ny * inch / sy, -nz * inch / sz]
        }
        specular 0.03  shininess 8  brilliance 1.1
    }
}

// -- Weatherboard ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// What goes on a gable.  Brick is what a wall is made of; a gable above it is boarded and painted,
// which is both what houses do and a welcome change of texture where the wall stops.

// A gable has to be a different color from the wall under it, and *which way* different depends on the
// brick.  Painting all three a cream was measured at gallery distance and the yellow stock house came
// back with a gable of 131, 127, 108 over a wall of 126, 126, 107 -- the boarding had simply vanished,
// while the same cream over red brick stood 30 clear.  A pale brick has no room left above it, so its
// boarding goes dark instead; the red keeps its off-white, and the grey a sage that sits 16 below it.
// One dark, one light, one middling, and each one chosen against what it is over.
//
// The `color` casts are load-bearing.  A bare `[r, g, b]` is a tuple, and the `-> color` on the
// declaration does not convert it -- so the shades below, which multiply this by a number, failed with
// "cannot multiply items of type NumberTuple to those of type Double".  `Color * double` is perfectly
// well supported; it was never a color.
function BuildSidingTint(variant) -> color {
    switch (mod(floor(variant), 3)) {
        case 0 { return color [0.32, 0.36, 0.31] }
        case 1 { return color [0.78, 0.76, 0.72] }
        default { return color [0.52, 0.56, 0.50] }
    }
}

// A lapped board, drawn from its shadow rather than from its color.
//
// `linear Y gradient` is a sawtooth: it climbs cleanly from nought to one over each unit and starts
// again, so one unit is one board and the color map is a cross-section of it.  Reading it from the
// bottom of a board upwards is reading it the way the light does: the bottom edge stands proudest of
// the wall and catches most, the face recedes as it goes up, and the last tenth is in the shadow of the
// board lapped over it.  Then the value drops back to nought at the next edge, and that discontinuity
// *is* the board line -- there is nothing to smooth out.
//
// The exposure is six inches, in the same inches the brick is laid in, so boards and courses are both
// real sizes and a taller house gets more of each.  Where the bottom course falls is left alone; siding
// is cut to the eave, so a part board at the wall top is what a house actually looks like.
primitive BuildSidingOf(variant, height, sy) -> material {
    board = height * 6 / 240

    return material {
        pigment linear Y gradient {
            [0.00, color BuildSidingTint(variant),
             0.55, color BuildSidingTint(variant) * 0.93,
             0.86, color BuildSidingTint(variant) * 0.84,
             0.90, color BuildSidingTint(variant) * 0.42,
             1.00, color BuildSidingTint(variant) * 0.42]
            scale [1, board / sy, 1]
        }
        specular 0.07  shininess 24
    }
}

// The same paint with no boards in it, for the gable's sloping edges.  Those belong to the *diamond*,
// which is turned a quarter, so a pattern read in its space would run its boards at forty-five degrees
// -- the one place in the gable where the boarded material would be wrong.  They are slivers under the
// roof, so flat paint is all they need and all they should have.
primitive BuildSidingFlatOf(variant) -> material {
    return material {
        pigment BuildSidingTint(variant)
        specular 0.07  shininess 24
    }
}

primitive BuildRoofOf(variant) -> material {
    if (mod(floor(variant), 2) == 0) {
        return material BuildRoof
    }
    else {
        return material BuildRoofRed
    }
}

// -- A window ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// An opening set back into the wall, a frame standing proud *around* it, and a sill under it that
// reaches further out than anything else and throws the small shadow that sells the whole thing.
//
// It is placed with its middle on the face of the wall, so the glass falls behind and the frame in
// front.
primitive BuildWindow(wide, tall, deep) -> group {
    return group {
        cube { material BuildGlass  scale [wide, tall, deep]  translate Z -deep * 1.4 }

        cube { material BuildTrim  scale [wide * 1.18, tall * 0.09, deep]  translate Y tall * 1.09 }
        cube { material BuildTrim  scale [wide * 1.30, tall * 0.10, deep * 2.2]
               translate Y -tall * 1.10 }
        cube { material BuildTrim  scale [wide * 0.10, tall, deep]  translate X -wide * 1.10 }
        cube { material BuildTrim  scale [wide * 0.10, tall, deep]  translate X wide * 1.10 }
    }
}

// A doorway: the leaf itself, a surround standing proud of it on three sides, a head across the top
// and a step at the foot.  The proportions echo `BuildWindow` on purpose -- a house whose door is
// trimmed differently from its windows reads as two houses.
primitive BuildDoorway(wide, tall, deep) -> group {
    return group {
        cube { material BuildDoor  scale [wide, tall, deep]  translate Z -deep * 0.5 }

        // The surround: up both sides and across the head, standing out from the wall.
        cube { material BuildTrim  scale [wide * 0.16, tall * 1.03, deep * 2.0]
               translate X -wide * 1.14 }
        cube { material BuildTrim  scale [wide * 0.16, tall * 1.03, deep * 2.0]
               translate X wide * 1.14 }
        cube { material BuildTrim  scale [wide * 1.42, tall * 0.055, deep * 2.4]
               translate Y tall * 1.03 }

        // The step, wider than the doorway and standing on the ground, which is what stops the door
        // looking as though it opens onto a wall.
        cube { material BuildTrim  scale [wide * 1.55, tall * 0.04, deep * 6.0]
               translate [0, -tall * 1.02, -deep * 3.0] }
    }
}

// -- A house -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The snow that lies on a pitched roof, which is nothing at all for three seasons of the four.  It is
// two slabs laid on the two slopes, a shade proud of them, so what shows is a white edge along the
// ridge and the eaves rather than a white roof.
primitive BuildRoofSnow(season, wide, deep, height) -> group {
    switch (season) {
        case 'winter' {
            // Laid a little way out along each slope's own normal.  Set at the slope's own offset
            // it disappears *inside* the roof, which is what the first attempt did -- snow has to be
            // pushed out, not merely placed.
            return group {
                cube { material BuildSnow  scale [wide * 0.84, height * 0.016, deep * 1.19]
                       rotate Z 45
                       translate [-wide * 0.56 - height * 0.026, wide * 0.56 + height * 0.026, 0] }
                cube { material BuildSnow  scale [wide * 0.84, height * 0.016, deep * 1.19]
                       rotate Z -45
                       translate [wide * 0.56 + height * 0.026, wide * 0.56 + height * 0.026, 0] }
            }
        }
        default { return group { } }
    }
}

// Snow on a flat top, which is what a parapet leaves.  It lies *across* the parapet rather than down
// inside it, and that is the whole point: snow lying inside a parapet is invisible from any camera
// standing on the ground, so a tower's season would do nothing at all in the scenes towers are
// actually in.  A cap on the wall shows as the bright line along the top of a snowy block, which is
// what you see from the street.
primitive BuildFlatSnow(season, wide, deep) -> group {
    switch (season) {
        case 'winter' {
            return group {
                cube { material BuildSnow  scale [wide, wide * 0.025, deep] }
            }
        }
        default { return group { } }
    }
}

// The shell of a house without its windows, so that a row can build one of these per dwelling and
// hang whatever windows that dwelling wants on it.
primitive BuildHouseShell(height, season, variant, wide, deep) -> group {
    walls = height * 0.50

    return group {
        // How it meets the ground.
        cube { material BuildTrim  scale [wide * 1.04, height * 0.03, deep * 1.04]
               translate Y height * 0.03 }
        cube { material BuildBrickOf(variant, height, wide, walls, deep, 0, 0.85)
               scale [wide, walls, deep]
               translate Y walls + height * 0.06 }

        group {
            // The end walls, cut as the top half of a diamond -- see the note at the head of this file.
            //
            // **The limiting box has to be thin, and it was not.**  The intersection's thickness in Z
            // was set by the diamond's own uniform scale, wide * 0.7071, which for a house six units
            // high is 1.44 either side: a gable 2.88 thick, centred just outside a face 1.80 out, so
            // it stood 1.42 clear of the house at both ends.  What that looked like was a solid
            // triangular prism jutting out over the upstairs windows -- and it was there from the
            // first render of this library, unremarked, because a gable seen straight on looks the
            // same whether it is a wall or a wedge.  It is only from an angle that it gives itself
            // away.
            //
            // A gable is a wall, so the box now sets the thickness and the diamond only the shape.
            intersection {
                cube { rotate Z 45  scale wide * 0.7071 }
                cube { material BuildSidingOf(variant, height, wide)
                       scale [wide * 1.2, wide, deep * 0.05]  translate Y wide }
            // The boarding goes on the *limiting* cube and not on the intersection, because a
            // pattern is worked out in the space of the leaf surface the ray actually met -- in an
            // intersection, one of the children.  The triangular face is the thin cube's, so that is
            // where the boards belong and that is whose scale has to be divided back out.  Writing a
            // pattern on the intersection instead reads it in whichever child is showing, which is
            // how the brick that used to be here came out two and a half times too long and twice
            // too tall.
                material BuildSidingFlatOf(variant)
                translate Z deep * 0.96
            }
            intersection {
                cube { rotate Z 45  scale wide * 0.7071 }
                cube { material BuildSidingOf(variant, height, wide)
                       scale [wide * 1.2, wide, deep * 0.05]  translate Y wide }
            // The boarding goes on the *limiting* cube and not on the intersection, because a
            // pattern is worked out in the space of the leaf surface the ray actually met -- in an
            // intersection, one of the children.  The triangular face is the thin cube's, so that is
            // where the boards belong and that is whose scale has to be divided back out.  Writing a
            // pattern on the intersection instead reads it in whichever child is showing, which is
            // how the brick that used to be here came out two and a half times too long and twice
            // too tall.
                material BuildSidingFlatOf(variant)
                translate Z -deep * 0.96
            }

            // The slopes, reaching past the wall so the eaves overhang and lay their shadow.
            cube { material BuildRoofOf(variant)  scale [wide * 0.80, height * 0.020, deep * 1.14]
                   rotate Z 45  translate [-wide * 0.56, wide * 0.56, 0] }
            cube { material BuildRoofOf(variant)  scale [wide * 0.80, height * 0.020, deep * 1.14]
                   rotate Z -45  translate [wide * 0.56, wide * 0.56, 0] }

            object BuildRoofSnow(season, wide, deep, height)

            // A gutter along each eave, and it earns its two boxes.  An eave is where a roof stops
            // being a roof, and a bare edge there reads as card: what a real one has is a half-round
            // running the length of it, standing a little below and a little out from the slope's
            // lip, throwing a line of shadow onto the wall head.  The tube is a tube rather than a box
            // because a gutter seen end-on is round, and the ends show wherever a house ends.
            for eave in [0, 1] {
                tube {
                    material BuildTrim
                    radius wide * 0.035 at [(eave - 0.5) * 2 * wide * 1.13, -height * 0.010, -deep * 1.14]
                    radius wide * 0.035 at [(eave - 0.5) * 2 * wide * 1.13, -height * 0.010, deep * 1.14]
                }
            }

            translate Y walls * 2 + height * 0.06
        }

        // **A chimney that reads as one.**  This was a cube of half-height 0.085 of the house standing
        // at y = 0.30 of it, which put its top at 8.31 against a ridge at 8.22 -- so a tenth of it
        // cleared the roof and the rest was buried in the slope.  From any distance it was a wart.
        //
        // A chimney is a stack and a cap: the stack wants to stand clear of the ridge by something
        // like its own width, and the cap is a thin slab a little wider than the stack, which is what
        // gives the top edge its line.  Set on the ridge rather than out on a slope, so it stands
        // upright out of the roof instead of appearing to lean out of it.
        group {
            cube { material BuildChimney  scale [wide * 0.115, height * 0.115, wide * 0.115]
                   translate Y height * 0.115 }
            cube { material BuildTrim  scale [wide * 0.145, height * 0.014, wide * 0.145]
                   translate Y height * 0.238 }
            translate [0, walls * 2 + height * 0.06 + wide * 0.72, -deep * 0.42]
        }

        // The downpipe: from the near-left eave to the ground, a hand's width off the wall.  One
        // vertical line on a face otherwise made of horizontal courses, and the eye finds it at any
        // distance -- which is why a street of houses without one reads as a model of a street.
        tube {
            material BuildTrim
            radius wide * 0.026 at [-wide * 1.06, height * 0.02, -deep * 1.02]
            radius wide * 0.026 at [-wide * 1.06, walls * 2 + height * 0.05, -deep * 1.02]
        }

        // The ridge cap: the strip of tile or lead that covers the join where two slopes meet.  It is
        // a small thing that reads at any distance, because it is the one horizontal line on a roof
        // otherwise made of two slopes going the other way.
        // The apex sits at wide * 1.12 above the wall top, not wide * 1.0: each slope runs
        // wide * 0.80 along its own 45 degrees from a centre at wide * 0.56, which reaches
        // 0.56 + 0.80 * cos(45) = 1.13.  Set at 1.0 the cap was inside the roof and showed nothing.
        cube { material BuildTrim  scale [wide * 0.07, wide * 0.07, deep * 1.16]
               rotate Z 45
               translate [0, walls * 2 + height * 0.06 + wide * 1.12, 0] }
    }
}

primitive House(height, season = 'summer', variant = 1) -> group {
    wide = height * 0.34
    deep = height * 0.30
    walls = height * 0.50

    return group {
        object BuildHouseShell(height, season, variant, wide, deep)

        for side in [0, 1] {
            across = (side - 0.5) * wide * 0.90

            object BuildWindow(wide * 0.13, walls * 0.20, deep * 0.06) {
                translate [across, walls * 0.55, -deep]
            }
            object BuildWindow(wide * 0.13, walls * 0.20, deep * 0.06) {
                translate [across, walls * 1.45, -deep]
            }
        }

        // **The door was a flat panel laid on the wall**, which is what a door looks like in a
        // drawing and not in a street.  What makes a doorway is the things around it: a surround
        // standing proud of the render, a step to meet the ground, and a head over the top.  All
        // three project, and projecting is the whole point -- each throws a small shadow, and it is
        // those shadows rather than the colour that say a hole was made in a wall.
        // Set so the foot of the door lands on top of the plinth, at height * 0.06, rather than
        // 0.18 below it.  The door always started inside the plinth; nothing showed while it was a
        // flat panel, but a step at the foot of it then had nowhere to sit and read as a nub.
        object BuildDoorway(wide * 0.11, walls * 0.30, deep * 0.03) {
            translate [0, walls * 0.30 + height * 0.06, -deep]
        }

        // A house set exactly square to the road is a house nobody built.
        rotate Y (random(variant, 61) - 0.5) * 7
    }
}

// -- A row -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Houses shoulder to shoulder.  They share a height and a depth, because a terrace was built at once,
// and differ in their walls, their roofs and their windows, because the people in them did not.
primitive Row(houses, season = 'summer', variant = 1) -> group {
    height = 3.6
    wide = height * 0.34
    deep = height * 0.30
    walls = height * 0.50

    return group {
        for house in [0, houses - 1] {
            group {
                object BuildHouseShell(height, season, variant * 13 + house, wide, deep)

                for side in [0, 1] {
                    across = (side - 0.5) * wide * 0.90

                    object BuildWindow(wide * 0.13, walls * 0.20, deep * 0.06) {
                        translate [across, walls * 0.55, -deep]
                    }
                    object BuildWindow(wide * 0.13, walls * 0.20, deep * 0.06) {
                        translate [across, walls * 1.45, -deep]
                    }
                }

                cube { material BuildDoor  scale [wide * 0.11, walls * 0.30, deep * 0.03]
                       translate [0, walls * 0.36, -deep] }

                translate X house * wide * 2 - (houses - 1) * wide
            }
        }
    }
}

// -- A tower -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Taller, and ended with a parapet rather than a roof: a low wall standing above the top floor, which
// is what stops a tall box from looking cut off.  The bays are regular because a building's windows
// are regular -- what varies between towers is how many bays and how tall a floor is, not where any
// one window landed.
primitive Tower(height, season = 'summer', variant = 1) -> group {
    wide = height * (0.15 + random(variant, 71) * 0.06)
    deep = wide * (0.72 + random(variant, 72) * 0.4)
    // A storey is a storey whatever the building is: a taller tower has *more* floors, not taller
    // ones.  Deriving the count from the height as a fraction gave every tower the same eight.
    floors = max(3, floor(height / 2.2))
    storey = height / floors
    bays = 2 + floor(random(variant, 73) * 3)

    return group {
        cube { material BuildTrim  scale [wide * 1.05, height * 0.018, deep * 1.05]
               translate Y height * 0.018 }
        cube { material BuildWallOf(variant)  scale [wide, height * 0.5, deep]
               translate Y height * 0.5 + height * 0.03 }

        // The parapet: a band standing above the top floor, and a course under it.
        cube { material BuildTrim  scale [wide * 1.04, height * 0.012, deep * 1.04]
               translate Y height * 1.03 }
        cube { material BuildWallOf(variant)  scale [wide * 1.02, height * 0.030, deep * 1.02]
               translate Y height * 1.06 }

        for level in [1, floors - 1] {
            for bay in [0, bays - 1] {
                across = (bay - (bays - 1) * 0.5) * wide * 1.6 / bays

                object BuildWindow(wide * 0.5 / bays, storey * 0.26, deep * 0.05) {
                    translate [across, level * storey + storey * 0.45, -deep]
                }
            }
        }

        object BuildFlatSnow(season, wide * 1.06, deep * 1.06) { translate Y height * 1.09 }

        rotate Y (random(variant, 74) - 0.5) * 6
    }
}
